{"parentUuid":"b80ddc86-ecdf-4067-a110-fb93690569a5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Why7Xidu6LJnjpZfLQzR58","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YV8oS5NuXn6moDus6r46fU","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"maximum empty rectangle formal proof verification packing problem"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2725,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2993,"cache_read_input_tokens":21280,"output_tokens":310,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2993},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2725,"output_tokens":310,"cache_read_input_tokens":21280,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2993,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox1t2vvdoJowFXygzFf","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2ac740f7-85c2-4183-8a98-924f8e4e1558","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:51:56.265Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"693ac492-9c0a-46c0-a162-6c4e2352077f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FTYnTMQ2fj45MitoF3uoow","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"formalization MIP integer programming optimality certificate Lean Coq Isabelle\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Lean Forward\",\"url\":\"https://lean-forward.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"Lean Programming Language\",\"url\":\"https://lean-lang.org/faq/\"},{\"title\":\"The Lean Theorem Prover (system description)\",\"url\":\"https://lean-lang.org/papers/system.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Coq-ky affair: A Primer on Coq — A Formal Proof Assistant | by Hassaanatif | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@hassaanatif/a-coq-ky-affair-a-primer-on-coq-a-formal-proof-assistant-493f83d814d7\"},{\"title\":\"From Zero to QED: An informal introduction to formality in Lean 4\",\"url\":\"https://sdiehl.github.io/zero-to-qed/03_theorem_provers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to the Coq proof-assistant for practical software veriﬁcation\",\"url\":\"https://www.lri.fr/~paulin/LASER/course-notes.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Verifying Integer Programming Results ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1611.08832\"},{\"title\":\"Automating Interactive Theorem Provers and Certifying Automatic Theorem Provers\",\"url\":\"https://homepage.divms.uiowa.edu/~viswanathn/compmain.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PhD thesis Formalization of Logic in the Isabelle Proof Assistant\",\"url\":\"https://matryoshka-project.github.io/pubs/schlichtkrull_phd_thesis.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Lean Workbook: A large-scale Lean problem set formalized from natural language math problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2406.03847v2\"}]\n\nI found search results related to proof assistants (Lean, Coq, Isabelle) and optimization certificates, though the results don't contain a highly specific focus on formalizing MIP optimality certificates. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Proof Assistants and Certificate Verification\n\nA trustworthy integration of algebra systems in proof assistants requires the validation of certificates produced by the algebra systems. This relates directly to your query about optimality certificates in formal systems.\n\nThe three major proof assistants you mentioned have different characteristics:\n\n**Lean**: Lean works with proof objects where user-written proof scripts and tactics are executed to produce an explicit term in the foundational logic that represents the proof, which is then passed to a small trusted component (the kernel) for verification.\n\n**Coq and Isabelle**: Coq is based on an expressive logic with dependent types and extensive algebra libraries, but it offers relatively weak proof automation; Isabelle/HOL features stronger automation and comprehensive analysis libraries but a weaker logic without dependent types.\n\n## MIP Optimality Certificates\n\nThe search also found research on verifying MIP results. For linear programming, duality theory tells us that an optimal primal solution and an optimal dual solution are sufficient to facilitate effective verification of optimality. In the case of checking infeasibility, a Farkas certificate will do. Therefore, verifying LP results is rather straightforward.\n\nHowever, the situation with MIP is drastically different. Small (i.e. polynomial size) certificates for infeasibility or optimality may not even exist. There are many forms that certificates could take: a branch-and-bound tree, a list of derived cutting planes, a superadditive dual function, or other possibilities for problems with special structures.\n\nThe search didn't return papers specifically focused on formalizing MIP optimality certificates in Lean, Coq, or Isabelle, though these topics are clearly related research areas.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"99d0fdba-257c-4a27-84dc-6c32621fc4d1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:02.550Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"693ac492-9c0a-46c0-a162-6c4e2352077f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b80ddc86-ecdf-4067-a110-fb93690569a5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CYES3TU2QBZevTgnuarFZe","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"verified solver\" combinatorial optimization OR-Tools certificate trust\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - Opzet/OR-Tools: Google's Operations Research tools: · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Opzet/OR-Tools\"},{\"title\":\"Satisfiability Modulo Theories for Verifying MILP Certificates - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0747717125001257\"},{\"title\":\"About OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/introduction\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Certifying MIP-Based Presolve Reductions for 0–1 Integer Linear Programs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.09277\"},{\"title\":\"A Multi-Stage Proof Logging Framework to Certify the Correctness of CP Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools - Google Optimization Tools download | SourceForge.net\",\"url\":\"https://sourceforge.net/projects/or-tools.mirror/\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Survey: Certifying algorithms: Computer Science Review: Vol 5, No 2\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1016/j.cosrev.2010.09.009\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - google/or-tools: Google's Operations Research tools: · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query on verified solvers, combinatorial optimization, OR-Tools, and certificate trust. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview of OR-Tools and Combinatorial Optimization\n\nOR-Tools is an open-source, fast and portable software suite for solving combinatorial optimization problems. OR-Tools was written in C++, with wrappers in Python, C# and Java.\n\n## Verification and Certification in Optimization\n\nThe search results reveal significant research on verifying solver correctness through certificates:\n\n**VIPR Certificate Format:** VIPR 1.0 is the first recently proposed general certificate format for answers produced by MILP solvers. VIPR 1.0 is a branch-and-cut general certificate format for MILP instances which handles optimality, infeasibility, and relaxed optimality bounds.\n\n**Proof Logging for Trust:** Proof logging is used to increase trust in the optimality and unsatisfiability claims of solvers. However, no constraint programming solver can practically produce proofs without significantly impacting performance, which hinders mainstream adoption.\n\n**Certifying Algorithms:** A certifying algorithm produces with each output a certificate or witness that the output has not been compromised by a bug, and a user can verify that the certificate proves the output is correct without having to trust the algorithm.\n\nThe search results indicate that while verification mechanisms exist for combinatorial optimization solvers, implementing them in practical systems like OR-Tools remains a challenge due to performance overhead concerns.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b9fcb196-4700-4098-8c10-a7df43e09272","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:02.860Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b80ddc86-ecdf-4067-a110-fb93690569a5","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"2ac740f7-85c2-4183-8a98-924f8e4e1558","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YV8oS5NuXn6moDus6r46fU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"maximum empty rectangle formal proof verification packing problem\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: An Absolute Placement ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.0557\"},{\"title\":\"Rectangle packing - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectangle_packing\"},{\"title\":\"A two-level search algorithm for 2D rectangular packing problem\",\"url\":\"https://web.itu.edu.tr/~keceli/optimizasyon/greedy.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Packing problems - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Packing_problems\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: Initial Results Richard E. Korf\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.aaai.org/ICAPS/2003/ICAPS03-029.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Approximation Algorithms for Rectangle Packing Problems\",\"url\":\"https://people.idsia.ch/~ingala/papers/thesis_rectangle_packing.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Which rectangle sets have perfect packings? - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214716021000270\"},{\"title\":\"C H A P T E R 3 APPROXIMATING COVERING AND PACKING PROBLEMS: SET COVER,\",\"url\":\"https://hochbaum.ieor.berkeley.edu/html/pub/Approx-Alg-book-ch3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Packing Problem - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/packing-problem\"},{\"title\":\"Rectangle Packing: An Incredibly Difficult Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.gorillasun.de/blog/rectangle-packing-an-incredibly-difficult-problem/\"}]\n\nI've completed a web search for \"maximum empty rectangle formal proof verification packing problem\". Here are the results:\n\nThe search returned several academic papers and resources about rectangle packing problems, though not all focus specifically on maximum empty rectangle formal proof verification. Here are the key findings:\n\n**Rectangle Packing Problem Overview:**\nRectangle packing is a packing problem where the objective is to determine whether a given set of small rectangles can be placed inside a given large polygon, such that no two small rectangles overlap.\n\n**Complexity and Verification:**\nIn the variant where small rectangles can have varying lengths and widths and should be packed in a large rectangle, the decision problem of whether such a packing exists is NP-hard. Since any solution to a perfect rectangle packing puzzle is a tight fit, its validity can be verified by a polynomial time algorithm, even though finding one takes superpolynomial time.\n\n**Maximum Empty Rectangle Approaches:**\nGiven a configuration with m rectangles packed, there may be many empty corners formed by the previously packed rectangles and the four sides of the container, and a candidate corner-occupying action places a rectangle at an empty corner.\n\n**NP-Hardness:**\nBin packing problems are at least NP-hard, and the best we can do currently is solving them with heuristic methods that find solutions relatively quick.\n\nThe search results show that rectangle packing is a well-studied computational problem with proven hardness results, though specific formal proof verification methods for maximum empty rectangles appear to be addressed in specialized academic literature beyond what these general references cover.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7110fcc6-e574-4506-ae1f-545fe0895fb2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:03.727Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2ac740f7-85c2-4183-8a98-924f8e4e1558","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"64638138-f4f5-468b-9d2e-9971843c0096","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017jCrz62JYbzemHq1kdDbRm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LLM Lean tactic proof writing Claude GPT 2025 APOLLO LEGO-Prover Llemma\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"APOLLO: Automated LLM and Lean Collaboration for Advanced Formal Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.05758v5\"},{\"title\":\"APOLLO: Automated LLM and Lean Collaboration for Advanced Formal Reasoning | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=fxDCgOruk0\"},{\"title\":\"[2505.05758] APOLLO: Automated LLM and Lean Collaboration for Advanced Formal Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.05758\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - lean-dojo/LeanCopilot: LLMs as Copilots for Theorem Proving in Lean · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/lean-dojo/LeanCopilot\"},{\"title\":\"LLM-Based Theorem Provers\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/llm-based-theorem-provers\"},{\"title\":\"Recursively Building Formal Proofs with Informal Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22819\"},{\"title\":\"Local Look-Ahead Guidance via Verifier-in-the-Loop for ...\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.825.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"LLMSTEP: LLM proofstep suggestions in Lean Sean Welleck\",\"url\":\"https://mathai2023.github.io/papers/40.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Lean Copilot: Large Language Models as Copilots for Theorem Proving in Lean\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2404.12534v2\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - wellecks/llmstep: llmstep: [L]LM proofstep suggestions in Lean 4.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/wellecks/llmstep\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about LLMs for Lean tactic proof writing in 2025:\n\n## APOLLO: Automated LLM and Lean Collaboration\n\nAPOLLO (Automated PrOof repair viaLLM and Lean cOllaboration) is a modular, model-agnostic agentic framework that combines the strengths of the Lean compiler with an LLM's reasoning abilities to achieve better proof-generation results at a low token and sampling budgets.\n\nApollo directs a fully automated process in which the LLM generates proofs for theorems, a set of agents analyze the proofs, fix the syntax errors, identify the mistakes in the proofs using Lean, isolate failing sub-lemmas, utilize automated solvers, and invoke an LLM on each remaining goal with a low top-K budget.\n\n**Performance Results:**\nOn the miniF2F benchmark, APOLLO establishes a new state-of-the-art accuracy of 84.9% among sub 8B-parameter models (as of August 2025) while keeping the sampling budget below one hundred. APOLLO raises the state-of-the-art accuracy for Goedel-Prover-SFT to 65.6% while cutting sample complexity from 25,600 to a few hundred, and general-purpose models (o3-mini, o4-mini) jump from 3–7% to over 40% accuracy.\n\n## LEGO-Prover\n\nThe LEGO-Prover pipeline relies on an informal proof sketch before attempting to generate the formal proof.\n\n## Other Key Models and Approaches\n\nDevelopers insert natural-language mathematical commentary between tactics, yielding large accuracy gains with models like Goedel Prover, DeepSeek Prover, and Llemma.\n\nLLEMMA is a language model shown to have few-shot tactic prediction and other related capabilities.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"98c98d30-9585-496a-bac8-648593aef869","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:04.085Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"64638138-f4f5-468b-9d2e-9971843c0096","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
