{"parentUuid":"7c2a33fc-0b2d-4e76-a682-4b9febd71dae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HybAKQnL3uvfKNm6b9BtoD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SJoBacewb9MCt8pwK2uzVz","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"AlphaEvolve DeepMind 2025 combinatorial optimization algorithm discovery"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2516,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5713,"cache_read_input_tokens":24273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5713,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox2t8QagBRYSmo9z488","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fd3afd21-57f3-4ebc-a2ef-7820ac023f8b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:10.531Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fd3afd21-57f3-4ebc-a2ef-7820ac023f8b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HybAKQnL3uvfKNm6b9BtoD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011ooWs27z6PsysUqWe373hZ","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"cake_lpr CakeML HOL4 verified SAT proof checker 2025"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2516,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5713,"cache_read_input_tokens":24273,"output_tokens":395,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5713},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2516,"output_tokens":395,"cache_read_input_tokens":24273,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5713,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox2t8QagBRYSmo9z488","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"38d37d49-2a37-40f1-a62f-e9cfe9808216","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:11.470Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fc28d046-d595-4ae8-aafe-d86b22139611","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Yb8RbDKAsTSYHyXcbKDtn9","type":"tool_result","content":"# PBLean: Pseudo-Boolean Proof Integration for Lean 4\n\n## What is PBLean?\n\nPBLean is a Lean 4 system that imports VeriPB pseudo-Boolean (PB) proof certificates, enabling formal verification of combinatorial proofs. As described in the paper, it \"parses kernel-format proofs and verifies them via reflection,\" bridging the gap between solver outputs and formal theorem proving.\n\n## Import Mechanism\n\nPBLean employs two complementary verification strategies:\n\n**Explicit Proof Terms:** The system constructs Lean proof expressions by composing soundness lemmas for each derivation step. However, this approach has severe memory limitations for large proofs.\n\n**Reflection-Based Checker:** The primary approach defines a Boolean verification function with formally proved soundness. The paper notes: \"Lean's native_decide then runs the checker as compiled code. This follows the pattern of Lean's bv_decide tactic and scales to instances that would exhaust memory under explicit proof construction.\"\n\n## Scalability and Performance\n\nPBLean demonstrates remarkable scalability improvements. Testing on Paley graph independence numbers shows striking differences:\n\n- **Explicit construction:** Times out (>60 seconds) for instances with p≥37\n- **Reflection-based:** Verifies Paley(101) with ~63,000 proof lines in approximately 3.5 minutes\n\nThe reflection approach successfully handles proofs with tens of thousands of steps, vastly outperforming direct formalization.\n\n## Verified Encodings\n\nA distinctive feature involves \"verified encodings\"—formalizing both constraint translation and correctness proofs within Lean. This eliminates the trust gap between solver verdicts and original problem semantics, producing theorems about actual combinatorial properties rather than abstract constraints alone."}]},"uuid":"a46012ec-cf53-499b-b668-484614f6837d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:16.848Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fc28d046-d595-4ae8-aafe-d86b22139611","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7c2a33fc-0b2d-4e76-a682-4b9febd71dae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AiEoZY8kp9KeWQxXU17txR","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"proof logging\" CP-SAT constraint programming optimality certificate 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"A Multi-Stage Proof Logging Framework to Certify the Correctness of CP Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol307-cp2024/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.21\"},{\"title\":\"Certified MaxSAT Preprocessing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-63498-7_24\"},{\"title\":\"CP: Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=572\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Constraint Programming Solver | google/or-tools | DeepWiki\",\"url\":\"https://deepwiki.com/google/or-tools/4.1-cp-sat-constraint-programming-solver\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"}]\n\nI found search results related to proof logging in constraint programming and optimization. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings from CP 2025\n\nThe search returned results from the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025), which includes recent work on proof logging:\n\n**Pseudo-Boolean Proof Logging:**\nA recent promising approach covering a wide range of solving paradigms is pseudo-Boolean proof logging, though mostly consisting of proof-of-concept works. Researchers have presented an efficient toolchain based on VeriPB and CakePB for formally verified pseudo-Boolean optimization. Proof logging has been implemented for the full range of techniques in state-of-the-art solvers RoundingSat and Sat4j, including core-guided search and linear programming integration with Farkas certificates and cut generation.\n\n**Multi-Stage CP Proof Framework:**\nProof logging is used to increase trust in the optimality and unsatisfiability claims of solvers. However, to date, no constraint programming solver can practically produce proofs without significantly impacting performance, which hinders mainstream adoption. A novel proof generation framework addresses this by introducing a CP proof format and proof checker, dividing proof generation into three steps: the CP solver produces a proof sketch called a scaffold, then a proof processor trims and expands the scaffold into a full CP proof, which is subsequently verified.\n\nThese results show active research into practical proof logging for constraint programming, though the search didn't specifically address CP-SAT optimality certificates.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"21b54b11-e225-4f65-ad25-9e5fce889859","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:17.586Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7c2a33fc-0b2d-4e76-a682-4b9febd71dae","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"38d37d49-2a37-40f1-a62f-e9cfe9808216","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011ooWs27z6PsysUqWe373hZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cake_lpr CakeML HOL4 verified SAT proof checker 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in ...\",\"url\":\"https://cakeml.org/tacas21.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Verified Proof Checking\",\"url\":\"https://cakeml.org/checkers.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - tanyongkiam/cake_lpr: LPR proof checking with CakeML\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/tanyongkiam/cake_lpr\"},{\"title\":\"Verified LRAT and LPR Proof Checking with cake_lpr Yong Kiam Tan\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2025/downloads/checkers/cakelpr.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7984575/\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cake_lpr:-Verified-Propagation-Redundancy-Checking-Tan-Heule/a23f1afd3c101e136f810e2ceb74dad295aa2b6f\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-72013-1_12\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML\",\"url\":\"https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/531575\"},{\"title\":\"Verified Propagation Redundancy and Compositional UNSAT Checking in CakeML | International Journal on Software Tools for Technology Transfer\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10009-022-00690-y\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Veriﬁed Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML Yong Kiam Tan1(B)\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-72013-1_12.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about cake_lpr, CakeML, HOL4, and verified SAT proof checking:\n\n## Overview\n\ncake_lpr is a verified LPR proof checker developed in CakeML that represents the first approach to formally verify propagation redundancy (PR) proofs on a succinct representation. The LPR format is backwards compatible with the existing LRAT format, but extends the latter with support for the addition of PR clauses.\n\n## Key Technical Features\n\nCakeML is a programming language deeply embedded in HOL4, i.e., its abstract syntax is represented as a HOL datatype and its semantics is formalized within HOL4. cake_lpr is verified using CakeML's binary code extraction toolchain, which yields correctness guarantees for its machine code (binary) implementation, and unverified extraction and compilation tools are removed from its trusted computing base.\n\n## Performance and Usage\n\nAs of February 16, 2025, cake_lpr performance was improved by around 5-10% and its compiled binary size was reduced by around 20%. The cake_lpr and cake_pb tools are used to audit solver outputs in the annual international SAT solving competitions.\n\n## Correctness Guarantees\n\nThe end-to-end correctness theorem for the CakeML LPR proof checker ensures that the machine code implementation always terminates normally according to CakeML's x64 machine code semantics.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3eb2bd3f-7dd0-45ae-b8e8-083d43df8d39","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:18.455Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"38d37d49-2a37-40f1-a62f-e9cfe9808216","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fd3afd21-57f3-4ebc-a2ef-7820ac023f8b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SJoBacewb9MCt8pwK2uzVz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"AlphaEvolve DeepMind 2025 combinatorial optimization algorithm discovery\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"AlphaEvolve: A Gemini-powered coding agent for designing advanced algorithms — Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/blog/alphaevolve-a-gemini-powered-coding-agent-for-designing-advanced-algorithms/\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaEvolve: A coding agent for scientific and algorithmic discovery\",\"url\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/DeepMind.com/Blog/alphaevolve-a-gemini-powered-coding-agent-for-designing-advanced-algorithms/AlphaEvolve.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaEvolve - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlphaEvolve\"},{\"title\":\"[2506.13131] AlphaEvolve: A coding agent for scientific and algorithmic discovery\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13131\"},{\"title\":\"AI as a research partner: Advancing theoretical computer science with AlphaEvolve\",\"url\":\"https://research.google/blog/ai-as-a-research-partner-advancing-theoretical-computer-science-with-alphaevolve/\"},{\"title\":\"Global Optimization for Combinatorial Geometry Problems Revisited in the Era of LLMs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.05943v1\"},{\"title\":\"Google DeepMind Introduces AlphaEvolve: A Gemini-Powered Coding AI Agent for Algorithm Discovery and Scientific Optimization - MarkTechPost\",\"url\":\"https://www.marktechpost.com/2025/05/14/google-deepmind-introduces-alphaevolve-a-gemini-powered-coding-ai-agent-for-algorithm-discovery-and-scientific-optimization/\"},{\"title\":\"Google DeepMind debuts algorithm evolving agent, AlphaEvolve • The Register\",\"url\":\"https://www.theregister.com/2025/05/15/google_deepmind_debuts_algorithm_evolving/\"},{\"title\":\"DeepMind AlphaEvolve in Production: AI Agent Recovering 0.7% of Google's Global Compute | Tech Bytes\",\"url\":\"https://techbytes.app/posts/deepmind-alphaevolve-production-deployment/\"},{\"title\":\"DeepMind Creates AlphaEvolve: An AI Agent That Invents - SmythOS\",\"url\":\"https://smythos.com/ai-trends/deepmind-creates-alphaevolve/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's comprehensive information about AlphaEvolve and its work with combinatorial optimization:\n\n## Overview\n\nAlphaEvolve is an evolutionary coding agent for designing advanced algorithms based on large language models such as Gemini, developed by Google DeepMind and unveiled in May 2025. It is powered by large language models for general-purpose algorithm discovery and optimization, pairing the creative problem-solving capabilities of Gemini models with automated evaluators that verify answers and using an evolutionary framework to improve upon the most promising ideas.\n\n## Combinatorial Optimization Applications\n\nAlphaEvolve was used to find and verify combinatorial structures that improve results on the hardness of approximately solving certain optimization problems. Notably, for MAX-4-CUT (partitioning into four sets), AlphaEvolve was deployed to search for a new gadget reduction and discovered an intricate gadget involving 19 variables with a complex weighting scheme, establishing a new inapproximability bound of 0.987.\n\n## Key Achievements\n\nAlphaEvolve found an algorithm to multiply 4x4 complex-valued matrices using 48 scalar multiplications, improving upon Strassen's 1969 algorithm. In 75% of cases AlphaEvolve rediscovered the best known constructions, and in 20% of cases it discovered a new object that is better than a previously known best construction, thereby improving the state-of-the-art.\n\n## Practical Impact\n\nAlphaEvolve was also used to optimize Google's computing ecosystem, improving data center scheduling heuristics that enabled the recovery of 0.7% of stranded resources, and optimizing TPU circuit design and Gemini's training matrix multiplication kernel.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f3c5e916-ec4f-412c-9054-3f16183e1b9d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:18.744Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fd3afd21-57f3-4ebc-a2ef-7820ac023f8b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
