{"parentUuid":"bbaa46e3-58c2-4d8d-8546-78a3d59aac28","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01WMGJeRj8nS18graNFafnrK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RZ4Yr98q5Fzq5mx8mo7bxF","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"formally verified\" branch and bound \"cutting planes\" optimality proof scale industrial"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1306,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6507,"cache_read_input_tokens":29986,"output_tokens":430,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6507},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1306,"output_tokens":430,"cache_read_input_tokens":29986,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6507,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6507,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox3wbYkW352XHYg4SXX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f51ff2d9-7ec3-4bd5-af40-c3d5f23986ab","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:26.827Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"74c1a5b6-1da5-4586-bf29-05d054c93a90","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TfYCYyiSVWmPRj3JaBLntT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Lean 4\" formal verification OR-Tools CP-SAT placement constraints grid\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Lean Programming Language\",\"url\":\"https://lean-lang.org/\"},{\"title\":\"If It Compiles, It Is Correct: A Practical Introduction to Lean 4 for Engineers\",\"url\":\"https://blog.lambdaclass.com/if-it-compiles-it-is-correct-almost-an-introduction-to-lean-4-for-zk-systems-and-engineering-2/\"},{\"title\":\"Lean 4 — Learn Functional Programming & Theorem Proving | lean4.dev\",\"url\":\"https://lean4.dev/\"},{\"title\":\"Theorem Proving in Lean 4\",\"url\":\"https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/Introduction/\"},{\"title\":\"Lean 4: Bridging Formal Mathematics and Software Verification\",\"url\":\"https://leodemoura.github.io/files/CAV2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Formal Verification of Blockchain Consensus Algorithms in Lean 4 | by Hideaki Takahashi | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@Koukyosyumei/from-paper-to-proof-how-we-can-formally-verify-blockchain-protocols-with-lean4-part-1-a7b3d0fc563f\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Rostering: Complete Guide to Constraint Programming for Workforce Scheduling - Interactive | Michael Brenndoerfer | Michael Brenndoerfer\",\"url\":\"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/cp-sat-rostering-constraint-programming-workforce-scheduling\"},{\"title\":\"Lean 4: Scalable Theorem Proving\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/lean-4\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - leanprover/leansat: This package provides an interface and foundation for verified SAT reasoning · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/leanprover/leansat\"},{\"title\":\"Why the open-source programming language is becoming a key tool to inject rigor and certainty into AI systems.\",\"url\":\"https://venturebeat.com/ai/lean4-how-the-theorem-prover-works-and-why-its-the-new-competitive-edge-in\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query. Here's a summary of the results:\n\n## Search Results Summary\n\nThe search returned information about three main topics:\n\n### Lean 4 and Formal Verification\nLean is an open-source programming language and proof assistant that enables correct, maintainable, and formally verified code. LambdaClass has been exploring Lean 4 as a tool for formal verification in zero-knowledge proof systems and in formalizing the kernel of Concrete. Additionally, the LeanSAT package is meant to provide an interface and foundation for verified SAT reasoning, with SAT tactics for automatically discharging goals about boolean expressions, using a SAT solver.\n\n### Practical Applications with Constraints\nA practical example of formal verification includes formally verifying an addition constraint that's common in ZK systems. This is exactly the kind of constraint verification that appears in zkVMs, and by proving it in Lean 4, we have machine-checked assurance that our constraint logic is correct—not just for test cases, but for all possible inputs.\n\n### OR-Tools CP-SAT and Placement Constraints\nThe search found information about CP-SAT in workforce scheduling contexts. Decision variables in CP-SAT rostering form a 3D grid where each cell represents a binary choice: assign employee e to shift s in time period t (value 1) or not (value 0).\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about integrations or combinations of Lean 4 formal verification with OR-Tools CP-SAT solver for placement constraints on a grid.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e350d8b6-d548-4d72-9c94-5d0523a0cfb5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:29.809Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"74c1a5b6-1da5-4586-bf29-05d054c93a90","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"5746603e-031b-4b3e-8055-7c6b85c20ab2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TrRPu1GfYNWTi1bsncnKUW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DeepSeek-Prover V3 V4 2025 2026 Lean theorem proving benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-Prover: Advancing Theorem Proving in LLMs ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.14333\"},{\"title\":\"[2405.14333] DeepSeek-Prover: Advancing Theorem Proving in LLMs through Large-Scale Synthetic Data\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14333\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-Prover-V2: Open-Source AI for Lean 4 Formal Theorem Proving | by My Social | 𝐀𝐈 𝐦𝐨𝐧𝐤𝐬.𝐢𝐨 | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/aimonks/deepseek-prover-v2-open-source-ai-for-lean-4-formal-theorem-proving-ab7f9910576b\"},{\"title\":\"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-Prover-V2-671B · Hugging Face\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-Prover-V2-671B\"},{\"title\":\"[2504.21801] DeepSeek-Prover-V2: Advancing Formal Mathematical Reasoning via Reinforcement Learning for Subgoal Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21801\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-Prover-V2 · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-Prover-V2\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-Prover: Advancing Theorem Proving in LLMs through Large-Scale Synthetic Data\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.14333v1\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-Prover-V2: Advancing Formal Mathematical Reasoning via Reinforcement Learning for Subgoal Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2504.21801v1\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek Launches Prover-V2 Open-Source LLM for Formal Math Proofs - InfoQ\",\"url\":\"https://www.infoq.com/news/2025/05/deepseek-prover-v2-formal-proof/\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-Prover-V2: Neural Prover in Lean 4\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/deepseek-prover-v2\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about DeepSeek-Prover versions and their Lean theorem proving benchmarks:\n\n## DeepSeek-Prover Versions and Benchmarks\n\n**DeepSeek-Prover (Original, May 2024):**\nThe model achieved whole-proof generation accuracies of 46.3% with 64 samples and 52% cumulatively on the Lean 4 miniF2F test, surpassing the baseline GPT-4 at 23.0% with 64 samples and a tree search reinforcement learning method at 41.0%. The model successfully proved 5 out of 148 problems in the Lean 4 Formalized International Mathematical Olympiad (FIMO) benchmark, while GPT-4 failed to prove any.\n\n**DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (April/May 2025):**\nIt is distinguished by merging informal and formal mathematical reasoning using a recursive pipeline driven by DeepSeek-V3 to solve difficult problems by dividing them into formal subgoals.\n\nKey benchmark results for V2:\n- The resulting model, DeepSeek-Prover-V2-671B, achieves state-of-the-art performance in neural theorem proving, reaching 88.9% pass ratio on the MiniF2F-test and solving 49 out of 658 problems from PutnamBench.\n- On ProofNet-test, an undergraduate-level benchmark, the 671B CoT model passed at a respectable 37.1% Pass@1024.\n- DeepSeek-Prover-V2 successfully solved 6 of the 15 AIME problems, while the general-purpose DeepSeek-V3 model solved 8 when using majority voting techniques.\n\n**Model Availability:**\nDeepSeek has released their Prover-V2 in two different model sizes: a 7B parameter version built upon their previous Prover-V1.5-Base featuring an extended context length of up to 32K tokens, and a much larger 671B parameter model trained on DeepSeek-V3-Base.\n\nNo V3 or V4 versions were found in the search results as of May 2026.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"203b8718-6f60-46bf-a054-e663bb6b4b10","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:31.848Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5746603e-031b-4b3e-8055-7c6b85c20ab2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bbaa46e3-58c2-4d8d-8546-78a3d59aac28","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GAR3nRwmCK7L8tFUiGXAia","type":"tool_result","content":"# Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\n\n## What It Does\nThis work presents an efficient toolchain for formally verified pseudo-Boolean optimization. It enables solvers to generate cryptographic proofs of their results that can be independently verified. The authors state: \"proof logging and checking performance in this much more expressive paradigm is now quite close to the level of SAT solving, and hence is clearly practically feasible.\"\n\n## Supported Solvers\nThe implementation covers **RoundingSat and Sat4j** specifically. It does **not** support CP-SAT or other solvers. The paper explicitly focuses on \"the full range of techniques in the state-of-the-art solvers RoundingSat and Sat4j, including core-guided search and linear programming integration.\"\n\n## Covered Techniques\nThe toolchain handles:\n- Core-guided search methods\n- Linear programming integration with Farkas certificates\n- Cut generation procedures\n\n## Problem Sizes\nThe document does not specify problem size limitations or benchmark parameters. While it mentions experimental evaluation demonstrating practical feasibility, concrete instance sizes are not disclosed in this abstract.\n\n## Key Tools\nThe implementation relies on **VeriPB** (proof verifier) and **CakePB** (formally verified checker) as the verification backbone."}]},"uuid":"06ac80c7-0e42-4328-87e2-0f5b938aa76d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:33.390Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bbaa46e3-58c2-4d8d-8546-78a3d59aac28","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f51ff2d9-7ec3-4bd5-af40-c3d5f23986ab","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"aaa9a46efbbbe9596","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RZ4Yr98q5Fzq5mx8mo7bxF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"formally verified\" branch and bound \"cutting planes\" optimality proof scale industrial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Complexity of branch-and-bound and cutting planes in ...\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10355223\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Complexity of cutting planes and branch-and-bound in mixed-integer optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339873490_Complexity_of_cutting_planes_and_branch-and-bound_in_mixed-integer_optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Complexity of branch-and-bound and cutting planes in mixed-integer optimization | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-022-01789-5\"},{\"title\":\"Complexity of branch-and-bound and cutting planes in mixed-integer optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.05023\"},{\"title\":\"Complexity of Branch-and-Bound and Cutting Planes in Mixed-Integer Optimization — II | Combinatorica | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00493-022-4884-7\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Planes from the Branch-and-Bound Tree: Challenges and Opportunities | INFORMS Journal on Computing\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/full/10.1287/ijoc.2022.1248\"},{\"title\":\"Branch and Bound in Mixed Integer Linear Programming ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.06257\"},{\"title\":\"Integer Programming: Branch and Bound | by Min Kyung Lee | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@minkyunglee_5476/integer-programming-branch-and-bound-015c064d27a3\"},{\"title\":\"Complexity of cutting planes and branch-and-bound in mixed-integer optimization\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/7672.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"23. Cutting planes and branch & bound\",\"url\":\"https://laurentlessard.com/teaching/cs524/slides/23%20-%20cutting%20planes,%20branch%20and%20bound.pdf\"}]\n\nI searched for information about formally verified branch and bound, cutting planes, and optimality proofs. The search results provide comprehensive information about the complexity theory and mathematical foundations of these optimization algorithms, but they focus primarily on theoretical complexity bounds and practical algorithmic aspects rather than formal verification in the sense of computer-assisted proofs.\n\nHere are the key findings:\n\n**Theoretical Complexity of Branch-and-Bound and Cutting Planes:**\n\nResearch investigates the theoretical complexity of branch-and-bound (BB) and cutting plane (CP) algorithms for mixed-integer optimization, studying the relative efficiency of BB and CP when both are based on the same family of disjunctions.\n\nStudies provide rigorous underpinnings to the empirically observed phenomenon that combining cutting planes and branching into a branch-and-cut framework can be orders of magnitude more efficient than employing these tools on their own, giving general conditions under which a cutting plane strategy and a branching scheme give a provably exponential advantage in efficiency when combined into branch-and-cut.\n\n**Optimality Proofs:**\n\nThe cutting planes generated during the algorithm give a cutting plane proof of length N for the inequality, and establishing lower bounds on the length of cutting plane proofs are a way to derive lower bounds on the efficiency of a cutting plane algorithm.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about formal verification systems (like theorem provers) applied to these algorithms at industrial scale. The results focus on mathematical complexity theory rather than computer-assisted formal verification methodologies.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8c34d5e7-16b9-4152-93f0-4f8d61d29a37","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:34.771Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f51ff2d9-7ec3-4bd5-af40-c3d5f23986ab","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
